<article class="env-lemma" id="030B">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/030B">Lemma <span data-tag="030B">10.37.10</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a domain. The following are equivalent: </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
The domain $R$ is a normal domain, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
for every prime $\mathfrak p \subset R$ the local ring $R_{\mathfrak p}$ is a normal domain, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
for every maximal ideal $\mathfrak m$ the ring $R_{\mathfrak m}$ is a normal domain. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We deduce (1) $\Rightarrow $ (2) from Lemma <a href="/tag/00GY" data-tag="00GY">10.37.5</a>. The implication (2) $\Rightarrow $ (3) is immediate. The implication (3) $\Rightarrow $ (1) follows from the fact that for any domain $R$ we have 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  R = \bigcap \nolimits _{\mathfrak m} R_{\mathfrak m}  \]
</div>
    <p>
       inside the fraction field of $R$. Namely, if $g$ is an element of the right hand side then the ideal $I = \{ x \in R \mid xg \in R\} $ is not contained in any maximal ideal $\mathfrak m$, whence $I = R$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>